<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Catalog>
    <!-- The player character's weapon.
         20 damage on a 1.0s period is 20 DPS, against the worker SCV's
         FusionCutter at 5 / 1.5s = 3.3 DPS. That ~6x gap is the design
         baseline's "a player clears far faster than an SCV does, at first";
         the SCVs are meant to win it back later through numbers and upgrades.
         Range 5 so the character can open on zerglings before contact, but
         not so far that walking away from a fight is free. -->
    <CWeaponLegacy id="Lob_Hero_Rifle">
        <EditorCategories value="Race:Terran"/>
        <Icon value="Assets\Textures\btn-upgrade-terran-infantryweaponslevel0.dds"/>
        <TargetFilters value="Visible;Player,Ally,Missile,Stasis,Dead,Hidden,Invulnerable"/>
        <!-- Spelled out rather than left to default to TargetFilters, because
             the unit now carries a second weapon that DOES court allies and the
             two must not be confused about which is which. -->
        <AcquireFilters value="Visible;Player,Ally,Missile,Stasis,Dead,Hidden,Invulnerable"/>
        <Range value="5"/>
        <Period value="1.0"/>
        <DamagePoint value="0.05"/>
        <Backswing value="0.5"/>
        <Effect value="Lob_Hero_Rifle"/>
    </CWeaponLegacy>
    <!-- The first White E.G.O. A probe, and it lives on the player character
         because that is the only thing on the map that can currently be walked
         up to a broken employee on purpose.

         TWO THINGS ARE BEING TESTED HERE and both are about filters.

         AcquireFilters permits Ally and REQUIRES Passive. Passive is the flag
         Lob_Panicked sets, so the only friendly this can ever pick up by itself
         is somebody who has already stopped functioning — which is the whole
         QoL ask: a crew member carrying White walks past healthy colleagues and
         stops for broken ones, and nobody carrying Red ever stops at all,
         because their weapon has no such filter. Whether the engine really
         auto-acquires an ally on the strength of a filter is the open question.

         A filter is one conjunction — required;excluded — so it cannot say
         "enemies, OR allies who are broken". That is why this is a SECOND weapon
         beside the rifle rather than a widened first one, which is also how
         stock does ground-and-air. The unit picks whichever weapon can legally
         hit what is in front of it.

         Range 5 and Period 1.0 to match the rifle: mending somebody costs the
         same attention as shooting something, which is the trade the mechanic is
         made of. -->
    <CWeaponLegacy id="Lob_Ego_Mercy">
        <EditorCategories value="Race:Terran"/>
        <Icon value="Assets\Textures\btn-ability-protoss-guardianshield-color.dds"/>
        <TargetFilters value="Ground,Visible,Passive;Self,Structure,Missile,Stasis,Dead,Hidden,Invulnerable"/>
        <AcquireFilters value="Ground,Visible,Passive;Self,Structure,Missile,Stasis,Dead,Hidden,Invulnerable"/>
        <Range value="5"/>
        <Period value="1.0"/>
        <DamagePoint value="0.05"/>
        <Backswing value="0.5"/>
        <Effect value="Lob_Ego_Mercy"/>
    </CWeaponLegacy>
</Catalog>
